      <article>
        <header>
          <h1>How to Pick a Giveaway Winner Fairly: A Step-by-Step Guide for 2026</h1>
          <p class="lede">
            Choosing the winner is the part of a giveaway people scrutinise most. One unclear
            moment, such as a name appearing from nowhere or an entry list nobody saw, is enough to
            start a "rigged" thread in your comments. This guide shows a simple, repeatable way to
            run the draw so anyone can see it was fair.
          </p>
          <div class="meta">
            <span>9 min read</span>
            <span>Category: Giveaways</span>
            <span>Updated <time datetime="2026-11-24">November 24, 2026</time></span>
          </div>
        </header>

        <figure>
          <img
            src="/eng-spin-the-wheel-picker.webp"
            alt="Phone screen showing a colourful spin wheel with usernames, mid-spin, for a social media giveaway"
            width="1200"
            height="675"
            fetchpriority="high"
            decoding="async"
          />
        </figure>

        <section class="quick-answer" aria-labelledby="qa-title">
          <h2 id="qa-title">Quick answer</h2>
          <p>
            To pick a giveaway winner fairly: publish your rules before entries open, build one
            clean entry list, <strong>show the full list on screen</strong>, pick the winner with a
            random method such as a <a href="/">spin wheel</a> while recording, check the winner is
            eligible, then post the video with your announcement.
          </p>
          <p>
            Fairness is about two things: the selection is random, and other people can verify it.
          </p>
        </section>

        <details class="toc" open>
          <summary>In this guide</summary>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#fair">What makes a draw fair</a></li>
            <li><a href="#rules">Write your rules first</a></li>
            <li><a href="#steps">The draw, step by step</a></li>
            <li><a href="#entries">Collecting entries by platform</a></li>
            <li><a href="#weighted">Weighted entries and the real odds</a></li>
            <li><a href="#screening">Duplicates, bots and fake accounts</a></li>
            <li><a href="#live">Live or recorded draw</a></li>
            <li><a href="#platform-rules">Platform rules and legal basics</a></li>
            <li><a href="#mistakes">Mistakes that hurt trust</a></li>
            <li><a href="#announce">Announcing the winner</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">FAQ</a></li>
          </ol>
        </details>

        <h2 id="fair">What makes a draw fair</h2>
        <p>
          People rarely doubt a winner because of the prize. They doubt the process. A draw that
          holds up has four properties, and a wheel helps with the last two:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>Rules fixed in advance.</strong> Eligibility, entry methods, dates and what
            counts as a valid entry are published before the first entry arrives.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>A complete, honest list.</strong> Every valid entry is on it and nothing else
            is.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Random selection.</strong> No person chooses the result. Each entry has a
            known chance of winning. Our explainer on
            <a href="/blogs/is-spin-wheel-truly-random">whether a digital spin wheel is truly random</a>
            covers how that works.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>A record anyone can check.</strong> A video of the full list and the spin
            beats a screenshot, because a screenshot only shows the end.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          A wheel is useful here because the process is visible. The list sits on screen, the
          slices are readable, and the result is not announced by a person but by a motion that
          anyone can see slowing down. For more on why that visibility affects how people react,
          see <a href="/blogs/psychology-random-decision-making">the psychology of random decision making</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="rules">Write your rules first</h2>
        <p>
          Most disputes can be settled by a sentence in your rules. Before entries open, put these
          in the post or on a linked page:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>The prize, its approximate value and who is providing it.</li>
          <li>Start and end dates, with a time zone.</li>
          <li>
            Exactly how to enter, and whether bonus entries exist. If they do, how many and for
            what.
          </li>
          <li>Who may enter: age limit, countries or regions, and anyone excluded.</li>
          <li>How many winners, and how they will be selected (for example, "a random draw on a spin wheel, recorded and posted").</li>
          <li>How and when the winner will be contacted, and how long they have to reply before a redraw.</li>
          <li>That winners are subject to an eligibility check.</li>
          <li>Any required platform wording (see the platform rules section below).</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="note">
          <p>
            <strong>Do not change the rules after entries open.</strong> If a mistake in the rules
            forces a change, announce it publicly and say what changed and why.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2 id="steps">The draw, step by step</h2>
        <ol class="steps">
          <li>
            <strong>Close entries and freeze the list</strong>
            Note the exact closing time. Entries that arrive after it are not included. Save a
            copy of the raw list before you touch it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Clean the list</strong>
            Remove duplicates, ineligible entries and obvious fake accounts according to your
            published rules, and keep a short note of what you removed and why. See the screening
            section below.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Load the wheel</strong>
            Open the <a href="/">free spin wheel</a>, paste your entries one per line, and
            check the count matches your list. Test one private spin first so nothing surprises
            you on camera.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Start recording, then show the list</strong>
            Begin a screen recording or livestream. Scroll through the loaded entries or show the
            total count before you spin. This single step does more for trust than any other.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Spin once</strong>
            Say the prize and the number of entries aloud, spin, and let it finish without
            interruption. Do not spin again unless your published rules say how and why.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Check eligibility</strong>
            Confirm that the winner met the entry requirements. If they did not, say so on camera,
            remove that entry from the wheel and spin again. Show the second spin too.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Announce with proof</strong>
            Post the winner's handle and the recording together, thank everyone who entered, and
            restate the claim deadline. A template is in the announcement section below.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Deliver and report back</strong>
            Send the prize, then add a short follow-up post saying it arrived. This closes the
            loop for people who wondered whether the prize ever shipped.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          New to the tool? Start with our guide to
          <a href="/blogs/spin-the-wheel-tool">what a spin the wheel tool is and how it works</a>.
          For lists that are too long to read on a wheel, see the FAQ below for a spreadsheet
          method.
        </p>

        <figure>
          <img
            src="/remove-after-spin.webp"
            alt="Close-up of a laptop screen showing a text list of usernames being pasted into a spin wheel entry panel"
            width="1200"
            height="675"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
          <figcaption>Pasting a cleaned entry list into the wheel, one name per line.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2 id="entries">Collecting entries by platform</h2>
        <p>
          The draw is the same everywhere. What changes is how you gather the list, and what to
          watch for.
        </p>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table>
            <caption>
              Getting an entry list from common sources
            </caption>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Source</th>
                <th scope="col">How to build the list</th>
                <th scope="col">Watch out for</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Instagram or TikTok comments</th>
                <td>
                  Copy usernames from the comments, or use a comment-export tool that complies
                  with the platform's terms.
                </td>
                <td>
                  Never give a third-party tool your password. Multiple comments by one person.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">YouTube comments</th>
                <td>
                  Copy commenter names from the video, or use a tool that works through the
                  platform's official interfaces.
                </td>
                <td>Entrants who did not meet a "subscribe" requirement.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Twitch or live chat</th>
                <td>
                  Ask viewers to type an entry word, then export the chat log. See
                  <a href="/blogs/streamers-use-spin-wheel">how streamers use spin wheels</a>.
                </td>
                <td>Bots and repeated messages from the same account.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Email list or form</th>
                <td>Export a CSV from your email provider or form tool and paste the names column.</td>
                <td>
                  Show names or initials only; never display full email addresses on camera.
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Events, schools, offices</th>
                <td>
                  Use ticket numbers or names. See
                  <a href="/blogs/school-raffle-spin-wheel">school raffles with a spin wheel</a>
                  and <a href="/blogs/hr-spin-wheel-employee-recognition-office-events">office events</a>.
                </td>
                <td>Selling tickets can turn a draw into a regulated raffle in some places.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h2 id="weighted">Weighted entries and the real odds</h2>
        <p>
          If your rules reward extra actions, such as tagging a friend or sharing a post, you can
          give those entrants proportionally higher odds. Either click the built-in <code>+</code> or
          <code>-</code> weight controls beside an entrant's row (1x to 20x) to keep one clean label on
          the wheel, or switch on duplicate entries and add their name once per earned ticket.
        </p>
        <p>
          The odds are simple to calculate: an entrant's chance is their number of entries divided
          by the total number of entries on the wheel. Here is a small example:
        </p>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table>
            <caption>
              Example: four entrants, seven total entries
            </caption>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Entrant</th>
                <th scope="col">Entries earned</th>
                <th scope="col">Chance of winning</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Ana</td><td>1</td><td>1 in 7 (about 14.3%)</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Ben</td><td>3</td><td>3 in 7 (about 42.9%)</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Chloe</td><td>2</td><td>2 in 7 (about 28.6%)</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Dev</td><td>1</td><td>1 in 7 (about 14.3%)</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          Three practical rules keep weighted entries fair:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Announce the weighting before entries open</strong> so nobody discovers it
            after the draw.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Cap the bonus.</strong> Without a cap, one person can earn dozens of entries
            and a large audience may feel the draw is not worth entering.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Make bonus actions verifiable.</strong> You must be able to check that a
            claimed share or tag really happened, or the bonus invites cheating.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Many hosts skip weighting altogether for simplicity. One entry each is the easiest
          system to defend.
        </p>

        <h2 id="screening">Duplicates, bots and fake accounts</h2>
        <p>
          A fair draw is only as fair as its list. Screen before you spin, never after, and apply
          the same checks to every entry:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Duplicates.</strong> Merge repeated names unless your rules award extra
            entries for them.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Eligibility.</strong> Check region and age limits, and whether the entrant
            completed every required step.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Suspicious accounts.</strong> Brand-new profiles with no posts or photos, or
            accounts that all posted the same text, may be bots. Your rules should say you may
            disqualify such entries.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Insiders.</strong> Exclude yourself, your employees and close family, and say
            so in your rules.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Keep a short log of removals. If someone challenges the result, "I removed 12 duplicates
          and 3 accounts outside the eligible region, per the rules" is a strong reply.
        </p>

        <h2 id="live">Live or recorded draw</h2>
        <p>
          Both are valid. Choose based on audience size and how comfortable you are on camera.
        </p>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table>
            <caption>
              Live draw compared with a recorded draw
            </caption>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Factor</th>
                <th scope="col">Live draw</th>
                <th scope="col">Recorded draw</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Verification</th>
                <td>Viewers watch it happen in real time</td>
                <td>Viewers rely on an unedited video</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Risk</th>
                <td>No retakes if something goes wrong on camera</td>
                <td>Can restart the recording, but must not cut the result</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Preparation</th>
                <td>Needs a prepared list and a tested wheel</td>
                <td>Same list, less time pressure</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Best for</th>
                <td>Larger audiences, streamers, events</td>
                <td>Smaller accounts, first giveaways, schools</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          If you record, post the footage without cuts between the list and the result. A
          screenshot on its own is the weakest proof, because it shows nothing about how the name
          was chosen.
        </p>

        <h2 id="platform-rules">Platform rules and legal basics</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>This section is general information, not legal advice.</strong> Giveaway law
          differs by country and by state, so check the rules that apply to you and your
          entrants.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Platform terms.</strong> Instagram's promotion guidelines say you must make
            clear that the promotion is not sponsored, endorsed or administered by Instagram, and
            that each entrant releases Instagram from responsibility. They also say not to
            encourage inaccurate tagging. Other platforms have their own wording, so read the
            current guidelines of every platform you use.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Sponsored giveaways.</strong> If a brand supplies the prize or pays you, the
            post is advertising. Disclose the relationship clearly, using a visible tag such as
            #ad or the platform's paid-partnership label, and follow the advertising rules where
            you live.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Do not charge to enter.</strong> In many places, combining a prize, chance and
            a payment turns a free draw into a regulated lottery. Keep entry free unless you have
            confirmed local rules.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Protect entrants' data.</strong> Use names and emails only for the giveaway.
            Do not show private details on camera.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Minors.</strong> Set a minimum age in your rules and, if the winner might be
            under 18, decide how a parent or guardian will receive the prize.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="mistakes">Mistakes that hurt trust</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Not showing the list.</strong> Without it nobody can tell whether the draw was
            complete.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Screening after the spin.</strong> Removing entries once the result is known
            looks like manipulation, even if you had good reasons.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Changing the rules midway.</strong> This is the quickest way to lose a
            community's goodwill.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Deleting "rigged" comments.</strong> It confirms the suspicion. Reply calmly
            and link to the full recording instead.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Announcing a winner without checking eligibility.</strong> If you must redraw
            after the announcement, do it publicly, quickly and on camera.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Going silent after the draw.</strong> Never confirming the prize was delivered
            leaves room for doubt.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="announce">Announcing the winner</h2>
        <p>A good announcement is short, specific and includes proof. It should contain:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The winner's handle, tagged.</li>
          <li>The recording of the draw.</li>
          <li>The prize, restated plainly.</li>
          <li>The claim deadline and what happens if it passes.</li>
          <li>A thank-you to everyone who entered.</li>
          <li>Any platform disclaimer your rules require.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="sample">
          <p>
            <strong>Sample:</strong> "The draw is done! From [number] valid entries, the random
            spin chose @[username]. Congratulations, you've won [prize]. Please reply within 48
            hours to claim it, or we'll redraw. Full unedited draw: [link]. Thank you to everyone
            who entered. This giveaway is not sponsored, endorsed or administered by [platform]."
          </p>
        </div>
        <p>
          Hosting something in person? Take the same structure to a party with our
          <a href="/blogs/spin-wheel-birthday-party-ideas">birthday party and baby shower wheel ideas</a>,
          or pick names with a <a href="/random-name-generator">random name generator</a> or a
          <a href="/blogs/name-picker-wheel-work-school">name picker wheel</a>.
        </p>

        <section class="cta" aria-labelledby="cta-title">
          <h2 id="cta-title">Ready to run your draw?</h2>
          <p>Paste your list into the free spin wheel, record the spin and announce with proof.</p>
          <a href="/">Open the spin wheel</a>
        </section>

