        <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <div class="faq">
          <details>
            <summary>Is a spin wheel draw legally valid for a giveaway?</summary>
            <p>
              A spin wheel is a method of random selection, but legal validity depends on how the
              whole promotion is run: written rules, eligibility terms, no payment required to
              enter unless local law allows it, and compliance with the platform's promotion
              guidelines. This guide is not legal advice, so check the rules where you and your
              entrants live.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>How do I handle duplicate entries?</summary>
            <p>
              Follow your published rules. If the rules allow one entry per person, remove
              duplicates before the draw. If extra actions earn extra entries, add the person's
              name once for each entry earned, up to any cap you announced. Apply the same standard
              to every entrant.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>What if the winner does not respond?</summary>
            <p>
              State a claim window in your rules before the draw, commonly 24 to 72 hours. If the
              winner does not respond in that time, announce the redraw publicly, remove the first
              winner from the list and spin again, recording the new spin as well.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>How many entries can I use on a wheel?</summary>
            <p>
              Our wheel accepts up to 500 names or ticket numbers cleanly on screen. For thousands of
              entries, number the rows in a spreadsheet and draw a random number instead, using a
              <a href="/number-picker-wheel">number picker wheel</a> for batches or the
              RANDBETWEEN formula in Excel or Google Sheets, then show the matching row on camera.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Should I tell the winner before announcing publicly?</summary>
            <p>
              For a live draw, the result is public the moment the wheel stops, so say in your
              rules that winners are subject to an eligibility check. For a recorded draw, post
              the video and announcement together, then contact the winner right away. Either way,
              the key is that the draw itself is shown.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Can I use the same method for a party or school raffle?</summary>
            <p>
              Yes. Use ticket numbers or names as entries, project the wheel on a screen so
              everyone can see it, and keep the rules simple and announced beforehand. Raffles
              that charge for tickets are regulated in many places, so check local rules first.
              Compare other options in our guide to
              <a href="/blogs/spin-wheel-vs-other-random-pickers">spin wheels versus other random pickers</a>.
            </p>
          </details>
        </div>

        <section class="takeaways" aria-labelledby="takeaways-title">
          <h2 id="takeaways-title">Key takeaways</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>Publish your rules before entries open and do not change them afterwards.</li>
            <li>Clean the list before the draw, and keep a short log of what you removed.</li>
            <li>Show the full list on camera, then spin once and record the whole thing.</li>
            <li>Check eligibility after the spin, and redraw publicly if needed.</li>
            <li>
              Weighted entries must be announced up front, capped, and based on actions you can
              verify.
            </li>
            <li>Announce with the video, then confirm delivery of the prize.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

